<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander Romance</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alexander Romance is a legendary account of the life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, falsely attributed to his historian Callisthenes and compiled in Greek by the 3rd century CE from earlier material. It makes Alexander the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nectanebo-ii">Nectanebo II</a>, the last native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Translated and adapted into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>, Persian, Ethiopic and many other languages, it became one of the most widely read stories of the medieval world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-romance.jpg" alt="Alexander Romance" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_romance._Armenian_illuminated_manuscript_of_XIV_century_(Venice,_San_Lazzaro,_424).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Armenian literary tradition begins in the 5th century, with a translation of the Bible, its oldest surviving text, and the Armenian <b>Alexander Romance</b>, translated in the same century.</blockquote><a href="/language/armenian">Armenian · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>…Humbaba alongside the Watchers and giants, and the quest of Alexander the Great for the Fountain of Life in the <b>Alexander Romance</b> was inspired by Gilgamesh's search for eternal youth, though there the water of life replaces the plant and a fish returning to life replaces the serpent.</blockquote><a href="/work/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>It introduced the <b>Alexander Romance</b> into Persian, turning Alexander from the negative figure of Zoroastrian writings into a positive one; the Iskandarnameh of Nizami and the Ayina-i Iskandari of Amir Khusrau owe much to it.</blockquote><a href="/work/shahnameh">Shahnameh · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Nectanebo II was the last native pharaoh, later turned into Alexander the Great's father in the <b>Alexander Romance</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/thirtieth-dynasty-of-egypt">Thirtieth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
